+# Connecting the passkey management UI
+
+This checkout is static and has no customer sessions, database, or authentication server. The UI uses clearly labeled sample passkeys when `GET /api/account/passkeys` returns 404 or a successful HTML fallback. Sample changes are saved in browser storage and never invoke WebAuthn signals. Authentication failures, network errors, and invalid API responses show errors rather than sample data.
+
+To enable real customer management, implement the following same-origin endpoints in the site's authenticated backend. Never trust a user ID supplied by the browser. Authorize every request against the customer session, verify ownership of each credential, protect mutations against CSRF, and serve responses with `Cache-Control: no-store`. Do not log credential lists or CSRF tokens.
+
+- `GET /api/account/passkeys`: return the customer's complete credential list.
+- `PUT /api/account/passkeys/:id`: accept `{ "name": "My MacBook" }`; trim and validate a nonempty name of at most 64 characters and persist the nickname.
+- `DELETE /api/account/passkeys/:id`: remove that customer's credential. Ensure the customer has an alternative sign-in method before allowing removal of their last passkey. Return a conflict if removal would lock them out.
+
+All successful responses, including mutations, must return the same complete snapshot. Return JSON rather than 204 for deletions. The snapshot returned after a mutation must reflect committed database state. Never paginate or filter the credentials in this response: omitted credentials can be hidden by a password manager after a signal.
+
+```json
+{
+  "authenticated": true,
+  "complete": true,
+  "rpId": "coffee.example.com",
+  "csrfToken": "session-bound-csrf-token",
+  "user": {
+    "id": "dXNlci0xMjM",
+    "displayName": "Alex"
+  },
+  "credentials": [
+    {
+      "id": "Y3JlZGVudGlhbC0xMjM",
+      "name": "My MacBook",
+      "provider": "iCloud Keychain",
+      "kind": "laptop",
+      "registeredAt": 1789171200000,
+      "lastUsedAt": 1793318400000
+    }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+Use the registration's original WebAuthn user handle and credential IDs, encoded as base64url without padding. The user handle is not necessarily the application's customer ID. `rpId` must match the RP used at registration. Times are persisted epoch milliseconds; `lastUsedAt` may be null. `kind` can be `laptop`, `phone`, or `key`; unknown values get the generic security key icon. Derive provider descriptions from stored registration metadata, with an unknown-provider fallback, and use them only as presentation hints.
+
+The client sends session cookies and `X-CSRF-Token` for mutations. Return 401 or 403 for unauthenticated/unauthorized requests, 404 for unavailable credentials, and an appropriate non-success response for other failures. The client renders server strings as text.
+
+For live accounts, the client feature-detects `PublicKeyCredential.signalAllAcceptedCredentials()` and calls it on initial load and after a confirmed deletion, using the authoritative complete snapshot. Renaming a nickname does not change the user's profile and does not call `signalCurrentUserDetails()`. An unsupported API or rejected signal does not undo an account change: the UI explains the limitation and offers a sync retry when appropriate. Retry reloads the full list before signaling. The Signal API updates supported providers; it cannot guarantee removal from every device or password manager.
+
+This UI does not implement passkey registration or primary authentication. Connect it to existing customer authentication before using real credentials.
